1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,887
How does a room receive light without a direct
view of a lamp, and why can a

2
00:00:04,887 --> 00:00:10,100
reflection lose an object outside the screen?
We will examine stored light,

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:15,720
reused screen information and light lists,
then transport through water and fog.

4
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:21,340
Understanding the data each technique keeps
also reveals what it cannot recover.

5
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:26,960
We begin with photon mapping, which collects
light arrivals before the game runs.

6
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:33,675
Previously we separated stored wall lighting
from shadows evaluated during play.

7
00:00:33,675 --> 00:00:37,956
Now consider light that continues after
meeting a surface.

8
00:00:37,956 --> 00:00:42,152
A red wall can reflect light toward a white
wall and tint it.

9
00:00:42,152 --> 00:00:46,979
Random paths starting at the camera do not
find every important route easily.

10
00:00:46,979 --> 00:00:50,989
Light passing through a refracting object and
concentrating on

11
00:00:50,989 --> 00:00:55,072
a small floor area can be difficult to reach
by ordinary sampling.

12
00:00:55,072 --> 00:00:59,718
Photon mapping changes the starting point. It
sends energy samples from emitters and

13
00:00:59,718 --> 00:01:04,428
records their positions and directions after
surface interactions. A computational

14
00:01:04,428 --> 00:01:09,202
photon represents a sample, rather than a
literal simulation of every physical photon.

15
00:01:09,202 --> 00:01:13,457
The emitter pass gathers arrivals. The camera
pass finds the visible surface,

16
00:01:13,457 --> 00:01:16,809
queries nearby arrivals and estimates outgoing
light there.

17
00:01:16,809 --> 00:01:20,612
Keeping those two jobs separate makes the
algorithm easier to follow.

18
00:01:20,612 --> 00:01:24,773
Our diagram places the emitter, glass and
floor at different depths.

19
00:01:24,773 --> 00:01:30,009
Gold paths show transport, green dots mark
stored arrivals, and a blue disk identifies

20
00:01:30,009 --> 00:01:34,742
the query neighborhood. These colors
distinguish movement from stored data.

21
00:01:34,742 --> 00:01:38,122
Counting dots alone does not determine
brightness.

22
00:01:38,122 --> 00:01:42,960
The estimate accounts for sample energy, query
area and surface response.

23
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:47,952
Increasing the number of emitted samples must
not create extra total energy.

24
00:01:47,952 --> 00:01:51,529
A fixed circular query has area pi times
radius squared.

25
00:01:51,529 --> 00:01:54,953
Doubling its radius makes the area four times
larger.

26
00:01:54,953 --> 00:02:00,812
This simple calculation explains why a surface
estimate depends on the neighborhood area.

27
00:02:00,812 --> 00:02:05,578
A smaller radius can preserve a narrow feature
but become noisy when too few samples arrive.

28
00:02:05,578 --> 00:02:09,538
A larger radius gathers more samples while
blurring neighboring features.

29
00:02:09,538 --> 00:02:12,880
Radius and sample count must therefore be
considered together.

30
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:19,187
The photon-mapping principle dates to research
in 1996. Its adoption by a game engine is a

31
00:02:19,187 --> 00:02:24,923
separate question. Unreal Lightmass provides
an example of precomputed static lighting,

32
00:02:24,923 --> 00:02:30,212
without implying that an engine implements
every feature of the original research.

33
00:02:30,212 --> 00:02:34,208
An important Lightmass question is where work
is concentrated.

34
00:02:34,208 --> 00:02:39,461
An importance region directs quality toward
the space used by the player. It does not

35
00:02:39,461 --> 00:02:45,232
simply erase everything outside the volume,
and the precise behavior is version dependent.

36
00:02:45,232 --> 00:02:49,647
A game can reuse baked results instead of
resolving the same static

37
00:02:49,647 --> 00:02:54,771
transport each frame. Changing a lamp or a
wall can invalidate those answers.

38
00:02:54,771 --> 00:02:59,342
Fast lookup comes with a tradeoff in how the
scene responds to change.

39
00:02:59,342 --> 00:03:03,024
This connects to the original script's concern
about baking cost.

40
00:03:03,024 --> 00:03:07,035
Finer spatial detail, more samples and
complicated transport add work.

41
00:03:07,035 --> 00:03:11,702
A reported number of hours is meaningful only
with its scene, settings and hardware.

42
00:03:11,702 --> 00:03:17,666
Testing baked lighting requires separating a
runtime intensity change from a rebuild

43
00:03:17,666 --> 00:03:23,792
of stored lighting. Otherwise an intended
static contribution can be mistaken for a bug.

44
00:03:23,792 --> 00:03:28,029
A bright pattern behind glass does not
identify photon mapping.

45
00:03:28,029 --> 00:03:32,109
A texture or another caustic approximation may
look similar.

46
00:03:32,109 --> 00:03:37,052
Implementation evidence and visible output
answer different questions.

47
00:03:37,052 --> 00:03:42,840
Progressive methods accumulate samples and
adjust query neighborhoods over iterations.

48
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:45,696
Shrinking the radius alone is insufficient;

49
00:03:45,696 --> 00:03:48,852
the update rule must also provide enough
samples.

50
00:03:48,852 --> 00:03:51,561
We now move from precomputation to coarse
spatial

51
00:03:51,561 --> 00:03:55,107
representations that update indirect light in
a changing scene.

52
00:03:55,107 --> 00:03:59,040
The quantity remains light, but its storage
and update schedule change.

53
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:06,759
Light Propagation Volumes, or LPV, represent
low-frequency directional light in spatial

54
00:04:06,759 --> 00:04:13,212
cells. Sharing a grid layout does not make all
volume techniques the same algorithm.

55
00:04:13,212 --> 00:04:18,597
Transporting a cell's light information to
neighboring cells approximates indirect

56
00:04:18,597 --> 00:04:24,056
illumination. Our diagram separates one
propagation step from repeated propagation,

57
00:04:24,056 --> 00:04:27,522
without treating each step as a complete
physical path.

58
00:04:27,522 --> 00:04:33,710
Direction matters because equal light arriving
from opposite sides illuminates different

59
00:04:33,710 --> 00:04:39,262
faces of a wall. A single brightness value
cannot represent that distinction well.

60
00:04:39,262 --> 00:04:45,010
A thin wall is difficult when the wall and the
spaces on both sides share coarse cells.

61
00:04:45,010 --> 00:04:51,242
The representation may fail to distinguish
blocked directions, producing light leakage.

62
00:04:51,242 --> 00:04:54,257
Finer cells increase storage and update work.

63
00:04:54,257 --> 00:04:58,895
Covering an entire large world at one fine
resolution can be expensive.

64
00:04:58,895 --> 00:05:03,532
Cell size is therefore a resource decision as
well as a quality setting.

65
00:05:03,532 --> 00:05:08,274
An engine demonstration establishes what that
implementation explored.

66
00:05:08,274 --> 00:05:12,338
It does not by itself prove use in a released
game. Presentation,

67
00:05:12,338 --> 00:05:16,402
engine support and shipped adoption remain
separate evidence.

68
00:05:16,402 --> 00:05:21,594
Fog will also use spatial cells later, but
density and directional illumination

69
00:05:21,594 --> 00:05:26,480
are different quantities. The important
question is what each cell stores.

70
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:32,481
Plausible lighting does not prevent thin
geometry from flickering in motion.

71
00:05:32,481 --> 00:05:37,362
Anti-aliasing addresses both spatial edges and
temporal instability,

72
00:05:37,362 --> 00:05:39,843
which must be evaluated separately.

73
00:05:39,843 --> 00:05:45,333
Supersampling renders more spatial samples
before reducing the image.

74
00:05:45,333 --> 00:05:51,813
It may increase surface and shading work,
unlike merely enlarging a completed image.

75
00:05:51,813 --> 00:05:57,851
MSAA evaluates geometric coverage at multiple
sample positions. It helps polygon boundaries,

76
00:05:57,851 --> 00:06:01,951
but does not automatically solve every form of
shading aliasing.

77
00:06:01,951 --> 00:06:05,083
Its scope depends on what is evaluated per
sample.

78
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:10,258
Image-based methods such as FXAA detect and
filter edges in a completed frame.

79
00:06:10,258 --> 00:06:15,983
They avoid additional geometry samples, but
their filtering can soften fine details.

80
00:06:15,983 --> 00:06:21,352
SMAA has spatial and temporal variants. A
shared family name does not

81
00:06:21,352 --> 00:06:27,093
imply identical inputs. Comparisons need the
exact mode and resolution.

82
00:06:27,093 --> 00:06:30,299
TAA reprojects previous samples into the
current frame.

83
00:06:30,299 --> 00:06:33,171
Motion information establishes correspondence,

84
00:06:33,171 --> 00:06:37,513
while jitter can provide samples at slightly
different positions over time.

85
00:06:37,513 --> 00:06:43,139
A newly revealed surface may have no valid
history. Reusing an old color there can leave

86
00:06:43,139 --> 00:06:48,843
a trail. More temporal information is useful
only when its correspondence is reliable.

87
00:06:48,843 --> 00:06:54,819
These principles return in reconstruction.
Remember the distinction between spatial

88
00:06:54,819 --> 00:07:01,360
samples, current-image filtering and history.
A still image cannot establish motion quality.

89
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:06,826
Forward and deferred shading organize material
and lighting work.

90
00:07:06,826 --> 00:07:11,405
Either can still waste work by testing every
light at every pixel.

91
00:07:11,405 --> 00:07:17,233
Forward+ and clustered approaches address
light selection as a separate problem.

92
00:07:17,233 --> 00:07:24,383
Screen tiles hold lists of potentially
relevant lights. Shading reads a tile's list

93
00:07:24,383 --> 00:07:31,533
instead of the global list, then performs the
remaining surface-level evaluation.

94
00:07:31,533 --> 00:07:37,057
In a teaching example, a scene has twelve
lights and one tile has three candidates.

95
00:07:37,057 --> 00:07:40,179
Pixels in that tile examine those three first.

96
00:07:40,179 --> 00:07:44,583
These counts illustrate selection rather than
a game benchmark.

97
00:07:44,583 --> 00:07:51,544
Light bounds can be represented as volumes
intersecting the space associated with a tile.

98
00:07:51,544 --> 00:07:54,200
Disjoint volumes can be excluded.

99
00:07:54,200 --> 00:08:00,063
The chosen bounds and depth range determine
how conservative selection is.

100
00:08:00,063 --> 00:08:04,627
A foreground object and distant wall may
occupy the same tile but need different

101
00:08:04,627 --> 00:08:08,783
lights. Clusters divide the view volume in
depth to reduce this overlap.

102
00:08:08,783 --> 00:08:15,831
We extrude the tile diagram into depth slices.
Light volumes intersect particular slices,

103
00:08:15,831 --> 00:08:22,423
and unrelated names leave each list. This
visualizes the actual selection question.

104
00:08:22,423 --> 00:08:25,845
Forward+ uses the selected list in forward
shading.

105
00:08:25,845 --> 00:08:29,890
Tiled or clustered lists can also support
deferred lighting.

106
00:08:29,890 --> 00:08:35,413
Light assignment does not replace a material
model or an indirect-light algorithm.

107
00:08:35,413 --> 00:08:41,778
Tile-based hardware organizes primitive and
pixel processing. Our current lists contain

108
00:08:41,778 --> 00:08:48,143
lights. The same word describes different data
and stages, and the approaches can coexist.

109
00:08:48,143 --> 00:08:53,910
Building lists costs work and memory. Large
light volumes may leave many candidates,

110
00:08:53,910 --> 00:08:58,797
while transparency and depth discontinuities
complicate assignment.

111
00:08:58,797 --> 00:09:01,360
The method is not universally faster.

112
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:07,137
After selecting lights, we combine values.
Image colors are often encoded

113
00:09:07,137 --> 00:09:12,113
for storage and display rather than
representing linear light directly.

114
00:09:12,113 --> 00:09:15,003
Adding encoded values changes the result.

115
00:09:15,003 --> 00:09:20,425
Decode color into a linear representation,
perform lighting and blending there,

116
00:09:20,425 --> 00:09:26,163
then encode the displayed output. That
sequence gives addition its intended meaning.

117
00:09:26,163 --> 00:09:31,430
In our numerical example, the linear midpoint
between black and white is 0.5.

118
00:09:31,430 --> 00:09:34,348
Its sRGB encoding is approximately 0.735.

119
00:09:34,348 --> 00:09:39,373
That is different from taking a midpoint of
0.5 in encoded storage values.

120
00:09:39,373 --> 00:09:44,323
The sRGB transfer function includes a linear
segment near black and a separate

121
00:09:44,323 --> 00:09:49,423
curved segment. A power of 2.2 is a useful
approximation, not the exact function.

122
00:09:49,423 --> 00:09:57,327
Not every texture represents color. Normals
and roughness encode data even when displayed

123
00:09:57,327 --> 00:10:04,923
as images. Their meaning determines whether a
color transfer function is appropriate.

124
00:10:04,923 --> 00:10:09,836
Automatic exposure can hide the cause of a
comparison. Hold lighting,

125
00:10:09,836 --> 00:10:14,249
camera and exposure fixed when testing color
interpretation.

126
00:10:14,249 --> 00:10:18,080
This separates a data error from a display
adjustment.

127
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:23,049
PBR describes how a surface responds to
incoming light.

128
00:10:23,049 --> 00:10:27,478
Finding that light and its visibility is a
different task.

129
00:10:27,478 --> 00:10:32,993
A better material cannot automatically restore
missing illumination.

130
00:10:32,993 --> 00:10:38,509
A microfacet model treats a surface as a
distribution of small orientations.

131
00:10:38,509 --> 00:10:42,922
Roughness changes how reflection is
concentrated or spread.

132
00:10:42,922 --> 00:10:45,893
Rough materials can still reflect light.

133
00:10:45,893 --> 00:10:50,275
Distribution, Fresnel and geometric masking
terms serve different roles.

134
00:10:50,275 --> 00:10:53,425
Treating them as one realism control obscures
errors.

135
00:10:53,425 --> 00:10:58,493
We focus here on their connection to transport
rather than repeating a material course.

136
00:10:58,493 --> 00:11:03,044
Image-based lighting combines directional
environment information with

137
00:11:03,044 --> 00:11:07,810
material response. Rough surfaces can use
filtered directional information,

138
00:11:07,810 --> 00:11:11,793
but a reflection probe does not know every
current local occluder.

139
00:11:11,793 --> 00:11:17,494
PBR, lightmaps and dynamic shadows are not
mutually exclusive. They can represent

140
00:11:17,494 --> 00:11:23,440
surface response, stored illumination and
current visibility within the same frame.

141
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:28,340
Bricks that appear raised may use different
representations.

142
00:11:28,340 --> 00:11:34,018
Normal mapping changes the shading direction
without moving the original vertices.

143
00:11:34,018 --> 00:11:37,173
The silhouette helps reveal that distinction.

144
00:11:37,173 --> 00:11:41,746
Parallax occlusion mapping uses a view
direction and height

145
00:11:41,746 --> 00:11:46,499
field to change texture lookup. It can suggest
self-occlusion

146
00:11:46,499 --> 00:11:51,073
and depth without generally creating a new
mesh silhouette.

147
00:11:51,073 --> 00:11:57,418
Displacement changes surface positions and can
use tessellation when more geometry is needed.

148
00:11:57,418 --> 00:12:03,763
Separating shading direction, lookup location
and geometry position makes their roles clear.

149
00:12:03,763 --> 00:12:10,520
Subsurface scattering allows light entering at
one location to emerge elsewhere.

150
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:16,603
It is distinct from emission and from
reflection at a single surface point.

151
00:12:16,603 --> 00:12:21,269
These surface techniques still depend on
illumination. In reflections,

152
00:12:21,269 --> 00:12:25,709
our first question is therefore what scene
information is available,

153
00:12:25,709 --> 00:12:28,720
rather than how impressive the material looks.

154
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:34,615
Screen-space reflections use an existing
frame's color and depth.

155
00:12:34,615 --> 00:12:40,154
A reflection direction is projected onto the
screen and searched there.

156
00:12:40,154 --> 00:12:44,603
Reusing the image also limits the available
information.

157
00:12:44,603 --> 00:12:51,065
With a unit normal and a defined incident
direction, reflection subtracts twice the

158
00:12:51,065 --> 00:12:57,983
normal component. Our diagram states the
direction convention before moving the arrows.

159
00:12:57,983 --> 00:13:01,478
Project points along the reflection ray,
compare their depth with

160
00:13:01,478 --> 00:13:04,974
the stored surface depth, and fetch screen
color at a suitable hit.

161
00:13:04,974 --> 00:13:08,033
The depth image normally records a visible
front surface.

162
00:13:08,033 --> 00:13:14,410
A depth crossing is not a complete
three-dimensional intersection test.

163
00:13:14,410 --> 00:13:18,936
Thickness tolerance and skipped intervals
matter.

164
00:13:18,936 --> 00:13:24,903
A comparison cannot manufacture hidden
back-surface information.

165
00:13:24,903 --> 00:13:30,178
Tilting the camera can move an object outside
the image while it remains in the world.

166
00:13:30,178 --> 00:13:33,225
The reflection then has no screen color to
fetch.

167
00:13:33,225 --> 00:13:36,643
The missing information is in the input, not
the scene.

168
00:13:36,643 --> 00:13:42,318
A surface hidden in the primary view may need
to appear in a reflection. A single visible

169
00:13:42,318 --> 00:13:47,760
depth layer lacks the complete scene
information needed to resolve that mismatch.

170
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:55,236
Fading a reflection near screen edges can make
failure less distracting. It does not recover

171
00:13:55,236 --> 00:14:01,473
the absent object. Concealment and information
recovery are different operations.

172
00:14:01,473 --> 00:14:05,951
A cubemap records the surroundings from a
probe position.

173
00:14:05,951 --> 00:14:11,252
It includes off-screen directions, but can
differ from a reflection

174
00:14:11,252 --> 00:14:15,823
at another position and may contain stale
dynamic content.

175
00:14:15,823 --> 00:14:20,254
Planar reflections render another view using a
reflected camera.

176
00:14:20,254 --> 00:14:23,577
They suit planar surfaces but add rendering
work.

177
00:14:23,577 --> 00:14:28,563
One reflected camera does not accurately
represent every curved surface.

178
00:14:28,563 --> 00:14:33,811
Ray-traced reflections query a scene
representation, allowing off-screen hits.

179
00:14:33,811 --> 00:14:38,009
They still require construction, intersection
and shading work.

180
00:14:38,009 --> 00:14:41,833
Extending information coverage is not
unlimited accuracy.

181
00:14:41,833 --> 00:14:48,505
Rough reflections consider a distribution of
directions. Sparse sampling introduces noise,

182
00:14:48,505 --> 00:14:53,009
while temporal filtering raises correspondence
issues again.

183
00:14:53,009 --> 00:14:56,763
Roughness, search and filtering are linked
choices.

184
00:14:56,763 --> 00:15:01,527
Hierarchical depth can accelerate search by
summarizing regions.

185
00:15:01,527 --> 00:15:07,628
Empty candidate regions permit larger steps,
while potential hits need finer checks.

186
00:15:07,628 --> 00:15:11,223
The underlying information is still screen
space.

187
00:15:11,223 --> 00:15:16,487
A useful test fixes camera conditions,
exposure and quality while an object leaves

188
00:15:16,487 --> 00:15:21,901
the screen. If its reflection remains, a probe
or ray-based fallback may contribute.

189
00:15:21,901 --> 00:15:25,360
The image alone does not identify the
implementation.

190
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:32,932
A large ceiling light is not equivalent to a
single point. Its angular extent interacts

191
00:15:32,932 --> 00:15:39,503
with the surface's reflection distribution.
Area-light shading considers both.

192
00:15:39,503 --> 00:15:44,149
LTC stands for Linearly Transformed Cosines.
It approximates a reflection

193
00:15:44,149 --> 00:15:48,287
distribution by transforming a simpler
integrable distribution,

194
00:15:48,287 --> 00:15:52,063
with a corresponding inverse transform of the
light polygon.

195
00:15:52,063 --> 00:15:55,779
We separate the reflection lobe from the
light's corners.

196
00:15:55,779 --> 00:15:59,345
Roughness and viewing angle change the
approximation.

197
00:15:59,345 --> 00:16:03,593
The light is not merely replaced by a
universal brightness number.

198
00:16:03,593 --> 00:16:07,321
An intervening cube requires visibility
handling.

199
00:16:07,321 --> 00:16:13,676
Integrating an area-light contribution and
determining occlusion are separate tasks;

200
00:16:13,676 --> 00:16:17,913
area-light shading does not automatically
solve shadows.

201
00:16:17,913 --> 00:16:23,563
A rectangle-light control does not prove an
LTC implementation.

202
00:16:23,563 --> 00:16:31,680
We explain the principle and verify the method
separately against engine-version evidence.

203
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:38,578
Water and glass involve refraction, reflection
and attenuation. We separate direction

204
00:16:38,578 --> 00:16:45,003
change at a boundary, energy allocation there,
and loss during travel through the material.

205
00:16:45,003 --> 00:16:51,382
Snell's law relates refractive indices and
angles measured from the interface normal.

206
00:16:51,382 --> 00:16:56,813
Our diagram varies the index and shows the
changed transmitted direction.

207
00:16:56,813 --> 00:17:02,689
Fresnel behavior changes reflection and
transmission with angle. Comparing grazing

208
00:17:02,689 --> 00:17:08,163
and near-normal views separates this
allocation from the refracted direction.

209
00:17:08,163 --> 00:17:12,108
Travel distance is another variable. Under
absorption alone,

210
00:17:12,108 --> 00:17:17,097
transmittance is an exponential of absorption
coefficient times path length.

211
00:17:17,097 --> 00:17:20,373
Thickness can therefore change the observed
color.

212
00:17:20,373 --> 00:17:26,585
Different absorption per color channel leaves
unequal amounts of red, green and blue.

213
00:17:26,585 --> 00:17:32,713
A single transparency value cannot capture
every distance-dependent color change.

214
00:17:32,713 --> 00:17:38,137
Unreal's Single Layer Water connects a water
layer with scene color and depth.

215
00:17:38,137 --> 00:17:41,754
It is an engine approximation rather than a
complete

216
00:17:41,754 --> 00:17:45,453
solution for arbitrary participating-media
paths.

217
00:17:45,453 --> 00:17:50,349
Screen-based refraction can fail on off-screen
or hidden information.

218
00:17:50,349 --> 00:17:54,041
A moving-object comparison distinguishes full
scene

219
00:17:54,041 --> 00:17:57,413
intersection from an approximate screen
lookup.

220
00:17:57,413 --> 00:18:03,218
Overlapping transparent layers also require
composition order. Each front layer

221
00:18:03,218 --> 00:18:09,520
changes the contribution behind it. Transport
and layer handling meet at the final pixel.

222
00:18:10,240 --> 00:18:16,626
Fog and clouds contain material along the view
path, not just at an ending surface.

223
00:18:16,626 --> 00:18:23,013
Rendering accounts for both loss along the
path and light scattered into the view.

224
00:18:23,013 --> 00:18:28,318
Absorption and out-scattering reduce radiance
along the beam;

225
00:18:28,318 --> 00:18:35,393
together they form extinction. In-scattering
adds radiance from other directions.

226
00:18:35,393 --> 00:18:42,328
Constant coefficients give an exponential
transmittance. Varying density requires

227
00:18:42,328 --> 00:18:49,073
integrating along the path. A homogeneous
example is not a complete model of a cloud.

228
00:18:49,073 --> 00:18:53,471
Our worked example uses extinction of 0.5 per
meter over two meters.

229
00:18:53,471 --> 00:18:57,946
Optical thickness is one and transmittance is
approximately 0.368.

230
00:18:57,946 --> 00:19:01,573
These are teaching values, not measured game
settings.

231
00:19:01,573 --> 00:19:08,071
Splitting that path into two one-meter
segments gives the same result when segment

232
00:19:08,071 --> 00:19:14,013
transmittances are multiplied. This supports
incremental accumulation.

233
00:19:14,013 --> 00:19:18,091
A fog overlay can approximate atmosphere using
distance or height.

234
00:19:18,091 --> 00:19:23,100
Volumetric lighting instead uses spatial
density, illumination and visibility.

235
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:26,320
Similar colors do not imply identical
calculations.

236
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:30,951
Divide the camera ray into segments. Evaluate
density,

237
00:19:30,951 --> 00:19:34,197
illumination and visibility within each one,

238
00:19:34,197 --> 00:19:40,023
then attenuate its contribution by the medium
between that segment and the camera.

239
00:19:40,023 --> 00:19:45,821
A rear contribution must not receive full
weight after an opaque front segment.

240
00:19:45,821 --> 00:19:52,053
Add it using current transmittance, then
update transmittance for the next segment.

241
00:19:52,053 --> 00:19:55,155
Segment opacity is one minus segment
transmittance.

242
00:19:55,155 --> 00:20:00,463
The corresponding color must represent the
defined scattering or emission contribution;

243
00:20:00,463 --> 00:20:05,083
substituting an arbitrary surface color is not
a physical volume calculation.

244
00:20:05,083 --> 00:20:10,868
In our example, the first layer has opacity
0.2 and the second 0.5. The second color

245
00:20:10,868 --> 00:20:16,653
is weighted by 0.8, and the remaining
transmittance becomes 0.8 times 0.5, or 0.4.

246
00:20:16,653 --> 00:20:20,599
The camera path alone does not determine light
visibility.

247
00:20:20,599 --> 00:20:23,914
Each sample also needs transport from the
source.

248
00:20:23,914 --> 00:20:29,203
We use different colors for the
source-to-sample and sample-to-camera paths.

249
00:20:29,203 --> 00:20:33,395
A longer path through the cloud attenuates
incoming sunlight.

250
00:20:33,395 --> 00:20:36,718
Deep regions and thin edges can therefore
differ.

251
00:20:36,718 --> 00:20:41,623
A bright edge is more than an arbitrary white
outline in a transport model.

252
00:20:41,623 --> 00:20:46,420
Coarse steps can miss density changes or
produce bands. More steps cost work,

253
00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:50,332
while jitter and history introduce temporal
considerations.

254
00:20:50,332 --> 00:20:54,023
Both sampling position and time handling need
evaluation.

255
00:20:54,023 --> 00:20:57,315
Early termination can skip distant
contributions

256
00:20:57,315 --> 00:20:59,688
after transmittance becomes small.

257
00:20:59,688 --> 00:21:05,200
Its threshold controls approximation error and
must be treated as part of the method.

258
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:11,101
Frostbite volumetric lighting and Horizon
cloud development illustrate

259
00:21:11,101 --> 00:21:15,706
engine approaches. Spatial grid processing and
procedural cloud

260
00:21:15,706 --> 00:21:20,723
density are separate decisions, even when
discussed in the same period.

261
00:21:20,723 --> 00:21:26,461
Density generation and lighting are different
stages. Animating a noise texture

262
00:21:26,461 --> 00:21:32,283
creates shape without automatically solving
sunlight visibility or scattering.

263
00:21:32,283 --> 00:21:39,108
Height fog, local volumetric fog and planetary
atmosphere operate over different

264
00:21:39,108 --> 00:21:46,713
representations and scales. Their apparent
similarity should not hide these differences.

265
00:21:46,713 --> 00:21:51,106
Atmospheric lookup tables reuse expensive
transport computations.

266
00:21:51,106 --> 00:21:54,084
Their representation differs from a lightmap,

267
00:21:54,084 --> 00:21:59,743
but both trade stored results for repeated
work. Updates still have conditions and costs.

268
00:21:59,743 --> 00:22:03,586
A reported millisecond cost belongs to
specific hardware,

269
00:22:03,586 --> 00:22:08,120
scene and settings. Wider coverage or more
samples need not retain it.

270
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:12,963
Process demonstrations and performance claims
require separate checks.

271
00:22:12,963 --> 00:22:18,555
Motion tests examine camera movement, sunlight
direction and density changes together.

272
00:22:18,555 --> 00:22:24,000
A single still cannot establish stability of
temporal accumulation or marching steps.

273
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:29,653
We asked how renderers handle light beyond
direct illumination.

274
00:22:29,653 --> 00:22:34,586
Photon mapping gathered arrivals, LPV
propagated coarse light,

275
00:22:34,586 --> 00:22:41,403
light lists reduced candidates, and reflection
methods used screen, probe or scene data.

276
00:22:41,403 --> 00:22:47,869
Water and fog required direction, distance and
scattering. These methods are not a

277
00:22:47,869 --> 00:22:54,613
replacement ladder; different inputs and
responsibilities can coexist in one frame.

278
00:22:54,613 --> 00:23:00,831
Ask what an option stores, when it updates and
which information is absent.

279
00:23:00,831 --> 00:23:07,840
Next we follow rays beyond the screen and
connect them to sampling, caches and Lumen.
